**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 10’s phase changes, diagrams, and exercises; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), phase changes and vapor pressure; [NIST Chemistry WebBook — evaluated properties and their sources](../sources/NIST-Chemistry-WebBook.lingua#r_7MMJR7YZKCS02QYACZM15GFQVP), a chosen phase-property record and its references.

A phase boundary summarizes equilibrium coexistence under chosen variables. Distinguish a pure-substance pressure-temperature diagram from a mixture's composition-dependent diagram. A region or boundary does not describe how quickly a real sample reaches it, nor whether a metastable state persists during an observation.

Vapor pressure concerns equilibrium with a specified condensed phase at a temperature. Boiling occurs when the appropriate equilibrium conditions and surrounding pressure permit bubble formation; evaporation can occur under other circumstances. Melting, sublimation, critical behavior, and a triple point have distinct definitions that should be connected to the diagram and actual species.

The approximate integrated Clausius–Clapeyron relation ln(p₂/p₁) = −ΔH_vap/R (1/T₂−1/T₁) assumes an appropriate ideal-vapor/condensed-volume treatment and nearly constant enthalpy over the range. It is a model-based estimate, not a universal interpolation formula. For invented ΔH_vap = 40.0 kJ mol⁻¹, raising temperature from 300 to 310 K predicts a pressure ratio of about 1.68 under those assumptions.

**Work:** explain paths through a phase diagram, identifying intensive variables and possible coexistence. Derive the approximate pressure relation from its assumptions, check the teaching ratio, and compare with identified evaluated data over a bounded range. Explain what would invalidate extrapolation.

Read the complete phase-change sections and the data source conventions. Mixture separations, crystal growth, distillation, drying, and materials processing will reuse this foundation with more specific phase and transport models.

